{"content":"The regression has been identified and fixed. In sqlparse/sql.py, IdentifierList.get_identifiers() had an inverted condition: it was yielding whitespace and comma punctuation tokens while skipping actual identifier tokens, contradicting its own docstring ("Whitespaces and punctuations are not included in this generator"). This broke _process_identifierlist in sqlparse/filters/aligned_indent.py, which relies on get_identifiers() to correctly align SELECT column lists etc. when reindent_aligned=True, causing the 24 reported 'format reindent aligned' regressions.

Fix applied:
```python
for token in self.tokens:
    if not (token.is_whitespace or token.match(T.Punctuation, ',')):
        yield token
```

Verified manually that sqlparse.format(..., reindent_aligned=True) now produces correctly aligned output for a basic SELECT/FROM/WHERE/AND query, matching expected formatting conventions (column list aligned under SELECT, keywords right-aligned).","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_finish","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed sqlparse regression: IdentifierList.get_identifiers() in sqlparse/sql.py had an inverted condition causing it to yield whitespace/comma tokens instead of actual identifiers. This broke aligned-indent formatting (reindent_aligned=True), which relies on get_identifiers() in _process_identifierlist to align SELECT column lists. Changed the condition from 'if (token.is_whitespace or token.match(T.Punctuation, ','))' to 'if not (...)' so it correctly skips whitespace/punctuation and yields identifiers, matching the function's docstring. Verified with manual test that sqlparse.format(sql, reindent_aligned=True) now produces correctly aligned output."}}]}
